docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-AUTH-02","criterion":"`release plan --from-release-commit` fails with E165 and writes no output file in any of these cases: HEAD is not the given commit; the commit neither adds nor modifies the decision file; it deletes no changeset `.md` file; a claimed package's manifest does not change to its claimed version; a claimed package's changelog is unchanged; an unclaimed manifest version changes; or a claimed package is not found.","is_error_case":true}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-AUTH-03","criterion":"A committed decision file whose digest does not match its entries fails with E166. Decision schema versions 1 and 2 are accepted, and any other version fails with an error naming the version found.","is_error_case":true}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-ORD-01","criterion":"A graph package's tag is created only after all selected registry publishes belonging to its canonical manifests succeed, and its GitHub release draft only after its tag. A `[[release.artifact]]` slot's upload follows the product's draft and gates the product's publish even when a different selected package owns the asset, and the release is published only after the draft and every upload."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-ORD-02","criterion":"A selected package publishes after the selected packages it depends on through runtime, build, optional or dev edges. A dev edge between two packages in the same dependency cycle does not order them, and a runtime, build or optional cycle fails with E104."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-PUB-04","criterion":"PyPI publish runs `python3 -m build --sdist --wheel --outdir dist/` in the package directory, falling back to `python` and failing with E170 when neither is on PATH. It then runs `twine upload dist/<normalized-name>-<version>*` with `--repository-url` for a configured index, adding `--skip-existing` only for the default index, `upload.pypi.org` or `test.pypi.org`."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-PUB-08","criterion":"PyPI observation fetches `<index>/<normalized-name>/` (default `https://pypi.org/simple`) with `curl` and `Accept: application/vnd.pypi.simple.v1+json`. In a 200 JSON body, any matching yanked file means absent; otherwise at least one matching non-yanked file means present, without checking an expected wheel/sdist set. A 404 or no matching file means absent, and a non-JSON body or other non-retryable status is indeterminate. Transient HTTP statuses use the observation retry policy."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-RERUN-01","criterion":"An operation whose result is already present and identical is recorded as already satisfied, and its effect is not repeated. For a registry version, stderr shows `warning: <package> <version> is already published; skipping`."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-RERUN-02","criterion":"After a run fails partway, rerunning the same command with no state file from the failed run performs only the missing operations and writes a receipt. A local annotated tag on the expected commit that is missing on the remote is pushed before the receipt is written, regardless of its annotation text."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-RERUN-04","criterion":"A receipt is written only when every operation ends `published` or `alreadySatisfied`, and otherwise the run exits 1 with no receipt file (E172 when operations remain incomplete). The receipt records `intentDigest`, the envelope (`orchestrationRevision`, `releaseSourceRevision`, intent and artifact-manifest digests), and each operation's outcome and provider evidence.","is_error_case":true}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-GH-07","criterion":"In the release workflow, `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` reaches only the `execute` job's execute step. The workflow's default permissions are empty, and the plan and build jobs receive no registry credential."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-PR-02","criterion":"`release-pr decide` fails in these cases: an invalid repository (E141), an unsafe branch name (E142), duplicate PR numbers (E143), a snapshot for another repository or base branch (E144), a managed-branch PR from a fork (E145), more than one managed PR (E146), a malformed SHA suffix (E147), or a PR headed at `<release-branch>--staging` (E154). Each of these carries its own code even when `--snapshot`/`--decision` is a file, inline JSON, or stdin. `release-pr verify` exits 0 when a fresh snapshot equals the decision's snapshot and fails with E148 otherwise.","is_error_case":true}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-PR-04","criterion":"The release-PR action runs the version command with `--emit-decision <decision_path>` (default `.callisto/release-decision.json`). It commits the result with GitHub's `createCommitOnBranch` on `<release-branch>--staging` reset to the base commit, and refuses unless the remote tree equals the locally staged tree. It then moves the release branch ref through the REST API and never runs `git push`. Before creating the commit, before moving the ref, and before any label or PR write, it runs `callisto release-pr verify` against a fresh snapshot and stops when verification fails."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-PR-06","criterion":"`callisto-action` and `setup-callisto` default `version` input `latest` installs the callisto release the action's own invocation is pinned to (`github.action_ref` resolved against `orin-dx/callisto`'s `callisto@` tags), falling back to the newest published release only when that ref is unset or matches no tag. An explicit `version` input overrides this resolution."}
docs/specs/release.json: {"id":"REL-PR-07","criterion":"`--format json` output of `release-pr verify`, `release-pr decide` and `release-pr commit-plan` (without `--out`) goes through the same envelope every other command's JSON report uses: a top-level `schemaVersion`, `command` and `dryRun`, alongside that subcommand's own payload fields unchanged."}
docs/specs/matrix.json: {"id":"MX-DISC-11","criterion":"`--package <name>` limits platformTargets and runtimeVersions to the one package it resolves to; a bare name resolves only when it matches exactly one package, and a qualified id such as `cargo/foo` selects the promoted package in that ecosystem."}
docs/specs/matrix.json: {"id":"MX-OUT-01","criterion":"platformTargets and runtimeVersions are keyed by the package's display id: the bare name, or `<ecosystem>/<name>` when packages in two ecosystems share that name, so a cargo crate and an npm package named alike get separate entries."}
docs/specs/matrix.json: {"id":"MX-ERR-07","criterion":"`--package` naming no registered package exits 1 with E102 naming it, and a bare name matching packages in several ecosystems exits 1 with E103 listing the qualified candidates.","is_error_case":true}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-AGG-02","criterion":"An entry name resolves to a workspace package by prefixed id (`cargo/foo`) or bare name; a bare name that matches packages in two or more ecosystems aborts the run with an ambiguous-name error (E103).","is_error_case":true}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-CAS-05","criterion":"Coverage: an exact spec covers only the equal version, a bare Cargo version is a caret requirement, a caret requirement never covers a prerelease, a Cargo `workspace = true` spec is tested using the root `[workspace.dependencies]` spec, and an npm `workspace:` spec always covers. A catalog or other untestable spec is treated as covering and never rewritten; a catalog spec emits a CatalogSpecNotRewritten warning."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-CAS-06","criterion":"A spec that no longer covers the new version is rewritten to it, keeping the operator and precision for Cargo and npm (`^1.2` becomes `^2.0` for 2.0.0), or to the exact version for npm when `preserve-npm-ranges = false`. A runtime, optional, build or peer dependent that is itself released in this run, for any reason, has every such spec on a bumped internal dependency raised to the dependency's new version the same way, even when the spec still covers it; a dependent that is not releasing keeps its spec unless out of range. `workspace = true`, npm `workspace:` and catalog specs are never rewritten by either rule, and a dev dependent's spec is rewritten only when out of range. A spec that cannot be rewritten mechanically, such as an npm range with spaces, `||`, a hyphen or a wildcard, is left unchanged with a RangeNotRoundTrippable warning."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-CAS-07","criterion":"A rewrite names the dependency as the dependent's own ecosystem spells it, so an npm dependent of a dual-manifest package with different Cargo and npm names is rewritten under the npm name. A Cargo dependency inherited with `workspace = true` is rewritten in the workspace root's `[workspace.dependencies]`, not in the member manifest."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-PRE-01","criterion":"`callisto pre enter <tag>` writes `.changeset/pre.json` with `mode` `pre`, the tag, every package's current version under `initialVersions`, keyed by its display id (`foo`, or `cargo/foo` when another ecosystem has a `foo`) and an empty `changesets` list, then stages the file with git; under `--dry-run` it prints the content and writes nothing. For promoted packages, an older bare key is assigned to the unique historical owner at the commit that added pre.json; if no unique owner exists, version fails with E245."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-PRE-03","criterion":"In pre mode, a bumped package's version is its `initialVersions` entry bumped by its severity plus `-{tag}.N`, where N starts at 0 and increments while the release part is unchanged (0.1.0 with a minor changeset and tag `beta` gives 0.2.0-beta.0, then 0.2.0-beta.1); PEP 440 packages use `aN` for tags `a`/`alpha`, `bN` for `b`/`beta`, `rcN` for `rc`/`c`/`pre`/`preview` (case-insensitive), and `.devN` for any other tag."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-PRE-06","criterion":"A malformed `.changeset/pre.json` (invalid JSON, a missing `mode`, `tag`, `initialVersions` or `changesets` field, a mode other than `pre` or `exit`, or an unparsable version) makes `version` fail with E114 and write nothing; an unreadable file fails with E115.","is_error_case":true}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-PRE-08","criterion":"A snapshot writes manifests and refreshes each active ecosystem's existing lockfile, unconditionally (there is no `--no-refresh-lockfiles` for `snapshot`), so a subsequent locked install resolves against the snapshot version; it ignores and keeps pending changesets, writes no changelog or `pre.json`, and stages nothing in git; under `--dry-run` it writes nothing."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-PRE-09","criterion":"For every workspace dependency whose current spec does not cover the snapshot version, `snapshot` rewrites that spec in the dependent's own manifest, using the dependent's ecosystem and the dependency's name as registered for that ecosystem; a spec that already covers the snapshot version is left unchanged."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-APPLY-01","criterion":"`version` writes the new version to every canonical manifest of a bumped package, so both manifests of a dual-manifest package move together."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-APPLY-08","criterion":"Outside pre mode, a successful `version` deletes the consumed changeset files, then stages every written manifest and changelog, `pre.json` changes, the existing lockfiles of affected ecosystems, and the changeset deletions (via `git rm --cached --ignore-unmatch`, which also succeeds for an already-deleted file)."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-APPLY-10","criterion":"`version --emit-decision <path>` writes the release decision JSON to `<path>` relative to the workspace root after the plan has been applied successfully: `schemaVersion` 2, a digest, and one entry per canonical manifest of each bumped package (ecosystem-qualified name, target version, reasons) sorted by package. Reason `kind` is `changeset`, `inference`, `fixedGroup` or `linkedGroup` (with `groupId`), `cascade` (with source package and edge kind, `peer` for peer escalation) or `preReleasePolicy` (with the prerelease tag)."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-APPLY-13","criterion":"After planning but before any write, `version` refuses with E121 when a canonical manifest's on-disk version differs from its version at `HEAD` and either: the workspace's changesets directory still has a pending changeset as of `HEAD` (not yet consumed by a commit); or, outside an active pre-release cycle (`.changeset/pre.json` absent), a canonical manifest is staged in the git index but not yet committed. Both are the signature of a `version` run that wrote and staged its changes but never committed -- the second covers a changeset that was only ever a working-tree file (added and consumed by `version` without ever being committed), which the first check cannot see. A repository with no commits yet, a manifest/changesets directory that didn't exist at `HEAD`, or an active pre-release cycle (which legitimately reruns `version` without a commit in between) is never refused this way.","is_error_case":true}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-APPLY-14","criterion":"By default, applying a plan refreshes each active ecosystem's lockfile that exists on disk: `cargo update --workspace` for Cargo; for Pypi, `uv lock` if `uv.lock` exists, else `poetry lock --no-update` if `poetry.lock` exists, else `pdm lock --update-reuse` if `pdm.lock` exists; for Npm, `npm install --package-lock-only --ignore-scripts` for `package-lock.json`, `pnpm install --lockfile-only` for `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `yarn install --mode update-lockfile` for `yarn.lock`, and `bun install --lockfile-only` for `bun.lock` or `bun.lockb`, each independently when its lockfile is present. `--no-refresh-lockfiles` skips this; `--refresh-lockfiles` is accepted but has no effect."}
docs/specs/versioning.json: {"id":"VER-APPLY-15","criterion":"A lockfile refresh command that exits non-zero aborts `version` with E208 naming the lockfile and the exact command that failed, instead of recording the failure and continuing.","is_error_case":true}

